Interesting question, because circles show up in a lot of systems that people experience as satisfying.

Some examples:

* Music — rhythms, grooves, repeating choruses, ostinatos, rounds.
* Breathing — inhale → exhale → pause → repeat.
* Walking — alternating gait cycles.
* Running — stride cycles.
* Swimming — repeated strokes.
* Rowing — catch, drive, recovery.
* Pedaling — continuous circular motion.
* Dancing — repeated step patterns, especially circle dances.
* Knitting, weaving, pottery — repetitive hand motions with visible accumulation.
* Conversation — revisiting themes from a new angle.
* Meditation/mantras — repeated phrases or sounds.
* Orbiting — planets, moons, electrons (in simplified models), recurring cycles in nature.
* Seasons — annual cycles that are familiar but never exactly identical.
* Heartbeats — rhythmic repetition sustaining life.

The common thread is that they combine predictability with variation.

Too much repetition becomes monotonous.

Too much novelty becomes chaotic.

The satisfying region is somewhere between:

Predictability
       +
Small variation
       +
Accumulation
       ↓
Flow

That also connects back to your “jam” idea. A good jam session isn’t just looping the same four bars forever. The circle provides stability while each pass introduces a slight variation. Jazz musicians call this “playing the changes”—the harmonic cycle repeats, but the improvisation evolves.

So your earlier line:

“Because we’ve been here before. We’re going in circles.”

could even be heard positively:

We’re cycling through a familiar structure, but each pass carries a little more than the last.

That’s much closer to a groove than to being stuck. A groove is, in a sense, a productive circle—repetition that generates momentum instead of stagnation.